<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gondophares</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 19–46 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Gondophares I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gondophares was the founder of the Indo-Parthian Kingdom, ruling in the early 1st century CE over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a>, Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, which he took from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takht-i-bahi">Takht-i-Bahi</a> inscription is dated to his 26th regnal year. Christian tradition, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-thomas">Acts of Thomas</a>, names him as the king who received the Apostle Thomas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gondophares.jpg" alt="Gondophares" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: [2] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gondophares.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
